          "slug": "hip-hop-dance",
          "name": "Hip-hop dance",
          "learnable": false,
          "summary": "The social and freestyle dance of hip-hop culture, from New York block parties to today's viral steps.",
          "description": "Hip-hop dance grew up alongside the music in the Bronx and Harlem in the 1970s and 80s, and every decade since has added its own party dances — from the Running Man and the Cabbage Patch through the Harlem Shake and the Dougie to the Griddy. Breaking, popping and locking are sister styles with their own histories; what people usually call hip-hop dance is the groove-based social style, where named steps are shared vocabulary and the point is to freestyle with them. Almost everything sits on a bounce or a rock, and the named steps are called \"grooves\" as often as \"moves\".",
          "tips": [
            "Learn every step on top of a bounce or a rock. Without the groove underneath, the steps look like exercises.",
            "The names are part of the culture. Knowing them is how dancers talk to each other in a cypher or a class."
          ],
