        },
        {
          "slug": "grooves",
          "name": "Grooves",
          "learnable": false,
          "summary": "The continuous body rhythm that every hip-hop step rides on: bounce, rock, and their variations.",
          "description": "A groove is how your body answers the beat when you are not doing anything in particular. Teachers usually split hip-hop grooves into accents — down, up, bounce and drop — which decide where the weight lands on each beat. A named step like the Steve Martin is a down groove; the Humpty Dance is a drop groove. Knowing which family a step belongs to tells you where to put the accent, and it makes new steps much faster to learn.",
          "tips": [
            "Before learning a step, ask where its accent is: down, up, or a drop.",
            "Practise grooves for a whole song without steps. It builds the stamina and the feel the steps need."
          ],
          "related": ["bounce", "rock", "two-step", "head-nod"]
        },
        {
          "slug": "party-dances",
          "name": "Party dances",
          "learnable": false,
          "summary": "Named social steps that spread through clubs, videos and songs — each one a snapshot of its era.",
          "description": "Much of hip-hop's vocabulary comes from party dances: steps with a name, often from a song, a TV character or a celebrity, that spread through clubs and music videos. The late 80s and early 90s produced the old-school canon (Running Man, Roger Rabbit, Cabbage Patch, Wop, Biz Markie, Bart Simpson); New York added the Harlem Shake and the Chicken Head; the South brought the Stanky Leg, the Dougie and the Walk It Out; and social media carried the Nae Nae, the Milly Rock and the Griddy worldwide. Dancers learn them as vocabulary, and they are the quickest way into the style.",
          "tips": [
            "Learn a few from each era. Mixing old-school and new-school steps is a big part of freestyle.",
            "Find the original video or song if you can. The feel of a step often lives in its source."
          ],
          "related": ["running-man", "harlem-shake", "dougie", "griddy"]
        },
        {
          "slug": "freestyle",
          "name": "Freestyle",
          "learnable": false,
          "summary": "Improvising to the music with the vocabulary you know, rather than a set routine.",
          "description": "Freestyle is where hip-hop dance lives. The steps on this page are vocabulary, and freestyle is using them to say something: choosing what to do on each phrase, changing grooves when the music changes, and adding your own flavour. Most teachers encourage students to freestyle a little every practice, even as beginners, because it is a different skill from learning choreography.",
          "tips": [
            "Pick three steps and freestyle only with them for a whole song. Limits make you creative.",
            "Listen for the changes: a new section or a drop in the track is a cue to switch grooves."
          ],
          "related": ["cypher", "musicality", "grooves"]
        },
        {
          "slug": "cypher",
          "name": "Cypher",
          "learnable": false,
          "summary": "The circle dancers form to take turns in the middle.",
          "description": "A cypher (also spelt cipher) is the circle that forms at parties, jams and events, with dancers taking turns in the middle. It is social, competitive and supportive all at once: you watch, you react, you go in when you feel it. It is where freestyle skills are tested and where the culture passes steps from one dancer to the next.",
          "tips": [
            "Watch a few rounds before you go in, and keep your round short at first.",
            "Hype the others up. Energy in a cypher is shared."
          ],
          "related": ["freestyle", "musicality"]
        },
        {
          "slug": "musicality",
          "name": "Musicality",
          "learnable": false,
          "summary": "Dancing what you hear: the beat, the snare, the vocals, the breaks.",
          "description": "Musicality is the difference between dancing to the music and dancing on top of it. In hip-hop that means knowing where the kick and snare fall, feeling the eight-count phrases, and choosing what to hit — a drum, a vocal line, a bass slide. Grooves keep you on the beat; musicality decides what you do with it.",
          "tips": [
            "Count in eights. Most hip-hop tracks are built in phrases of four or eight eight-counts.",
            "Try dancing only the snare for a whole verse, then only the vocals."
          ],
          "related": ["grooves", "freestyle", "bounce"]
        }
      ]
